<article class="prose">
  <!-- The page in five lines, and a way into it: it is long, and most readers want one part (round fifty-eight). -->
  <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-h">
    <h2 id="brief-h">In brief</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each species page is built ahead of time from public data (GBIF, Kew's WCVP, CHELSA, NASA POWER, iNaturalist, Wikimedia Commons, Wikipedia, OpenAlex), and every figure names its source.</li>
      <li>Nothing on a species page is written by a person or a language model: a sentence is a quoted passage, a figure with its source, or a labelled reading of a fixed rule.</li>
      <li>A source that did not answer is said as "not checked", never as "none".</li>
      <li>Your own plants are kept in this browser, sent nowhere unless you turn on sync, and then encrypted with a key only you hold.</li>
      <li>No accounts, no analytics, no third-party scripts; the code is open.</li>
    </ul>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#sources">Sources</a> · <a href="#list">Which species</a> · <a href="#refusal">Refusals</a> · <a href="#records">Records</a> · <a href="#marker">Map marker</a> · <a href="#climate">Climate across the range</a> · <a href="#year">Seasons</a> · <a href="#floor">Cold floor</a> · <a href="#archetypes">Archetypes</a> · <a href="#notes">In short</a> · <a href="#related">Similar climate</a> · <a href="#collection">Your collection</a> · <a href="#privacy">Privacy</a> · <a href="#glossary">Glossary</a> · <a href="#source">Source code</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <h2 id="sources">What comes from where</h2>
  <p>A species page is assembled from public sources, each named on the page where its data appears, with the licence it came under. The name and its synonyms, family and order come from the <b>GBIF Backbone Taxonomy</b>; a name the backbone holds as a synonym is followed to the species it accepts (and an accepted subspecies or variety one step further, to its species), and the page says which name was asked for. The native range comes from the <b>World Checklist of Vascular Plants</b> at Kew, read from Kew's files; a name WCVP lists more than once (a homonym) is told apart by authorship, and when authorship does not decide, the page says the name is unresolved and attaches no range. Regions where WCVP records the plant as extinct are shown labelled as such and are never part of the range records are tested against. Where WCVP has no entry the app falls back to national checklists and says so, at country level only. Kew's own one-line descriptions of a species, its life form and climate class, are quoted on the page and credited to WCVP, RBG Kew; nothing is derived from them. Occurrence records come from <b>GBIF</b>. Monthly climate normals come from <b>CHELSA V2.1</b> (1981–2010, 30 arc-second), packed to a 0.05° grid: day and night temperature, rainfall, solar radiation, relative humidity, vapour-pressure deficit and wind. Elevation comes from <b>ETOPO 2022</b> on the same grid. Daily temperature extremes come from <b>NASA POWER</b> (1981 onward). Photographs come first from the media that <b>GBIF</b>'s occurrence download carries (research-grade iNaturalist observations reach GBIF this way, and so do other observation datasets), then from <b>iNaturalist</b> directly for the cultivated set and for species the download gave few of, then from <b>Wikimedia Commons</b>; every photograph is restricted by its own licence to CC0, CC BY and CC BY-SA, whatever dataset it came through, and each carries its licence, and its author where the source names one (a CC0 photograph without one says so; under CC BY and CC BY-SA a photograph without an author is not published). The summary is the opening of <b>Wikipedia</b>'s article, four sentences at most, quoted as written, kept visibly separate and credited, with a link where more followed. Recent literature comes from <b>OpenAlex</b>.</p>
  <h2 id="list">Which species are here</h2>
  <p>The species list was derived, not chosen by hand: every accepted species, as WCVP lists them, of 172 collector genera (the cacti, the mesembs, the stapeliads and caudiciforms, the winter-growing bulbs and their like; the list is <code>scripts/specialist-genera.txt</code> in the source), plus the 2,500 species most often recorded as cultivated on iNaturalist across every kind of grower, resolved to accepted names, less the cereal, turf and timber families. The very large genera are not taken whole, and only the species of theirs that reached the cultivated count are here: Aloe (9 species, of the several hundred WCVP accepts), Euphorbia, Senecio, Oxalis, Dioscorea, Peperomia, Hoya, Ceropegia, Ornithogalum, Gladiolus, Moraea and Pelargonium. So a species that is not here is not a statement about the species, only about the list, and the list can be extended by rebuilding with more names (the README says how).</p>

  <p>Nothing on a species page is written by a person or a language model. The cultivation sheet and the "In short" paragraph are figures with their sources and the readings two fixed rules make of them, and each row says which figure it rests on. No row says what a plant does, wants or tolerates, or what to do to it.</p>

  <h2 id="refusal">A refusal is not an absence</h2>
  <p>Sources rate-limit, time out and go down. When one does, the page says "not checked" for that section rather than showing an empty list, because "we could not ask" and "there is nothing" are different facts and a grower reading the page needs to know which. Wherever there is no room for the sentence, the mark is one dashed pill reading "not checked", the same on every page, and tapping it says which source, and when. The corpus build keeps what a source gave last time when it refuses this time, and records the date it was carried from. What is carried is exact: literature, the summary and photographs, each per source, so a refused iNaturalist does not discard the Commons photographs the same build got; and the range, the records, the map marker and the climate as one snapshot when the occurrence or distribution source refuses, never piecemeal, because a range from one build and records from another would be a page no build made. The same rule applies in the other direction: a species with no habitat climate on file gets no derived climate rows, and a hybrid gets none at all, since it has no habitat.</p>

  <h2 id="records">Which records count</h2>
  <p>A species' records on GBIF include herbarium sheets with rounded coordinates, garden plants, and specimens filed under the wrong country. The app takes every georeferenced record with no flagged geospatial issue, drops living specimens (something somebody planted) and records marked introduced or managed, and tests each against the native range from WCVP, discarding records outside it. The range has to be WCVP's, with native status stated: a national checklist that only reports a plant present in a country cannot tell a wild record from a garden one, so for a name WCVP does not carry the page shows the records on a map, says the range is unverified, and derives no marker, no climate and no sheet from them. Restricted-licence records (CC BY-NC) inform the marker and the climate like any other; only openly licensed records are published on the map, in the data, or as the marker itself. Three agreeing records are enough for a narrow endemic; under a dozen the page says the evidence is thin.</p>

  <h2 id="marker">The map marker</h2>
  <p>The marker on the map is where the records are densest, and it decides nothing else. Records are binned at 1°, and the bin with most neighbours wins if it holds at least half the records or twice the runner-up; if no bin dominates at 1° the test repeats at 2° and 4°, and the densest 4° block stands when none dominates even there. Inside the winning cluster the densest 1° population is found again, and the marker is the openly licensed record nearest its middle, so the pin is somewhere the plant has been seen. When no openly licensed record lies in the population, the marker is the centre of the nearest tenth-of-a-degree cell instead, so that a restricted record's coordinates are never published; the page says which kind of point it is. The climate is not read at the marker.</p>

  <!-- The glossary's plain words through this page: "across the range", "a typical spot in the range", "plant", "sync key", "hash group", reading the log rather than folding it, and "this version of the app" (round fifty-eight; the accessibility review). -->
  <h2 id="climate">The climate across the range</h2>
  <p>The habitat climate is read across every in-range record, not at one point. Each record placed to within 10 km (or, with no stated uncertainty, a person's observation with coordinates to three decimals, the way a phone records them; a herbarium sheet placed at a locality's centre with no figure, and every vaguer record, stays on the map and off the climate) names one 0.05° cell of the packed CHELSA grid, and each distinct cell is read once, so a site visited a hundred times is one cell of climate and not a hundred votes. A cell at sea or on ice is dropped and counted: land is the grid's elevation layer, which averages only land pixels, so a cell with no land in it has no elevation (CHELSA itself carries temperatures over the ocean, so figures alone are no test); the few basins whose whole cell lies below sea level read as sea. A species recorded in strength on both sides of the equator (three or more cells poleward of 10° on the smaller side, a fifth of the cells that far from the line) takes its year from the larger side alone, since a January median over both is a month no place has; cells within 10° of the equator, where there is no temperature season to reverse, stay in either way, and the page says what was left out. Three land cells are the minimum; fewer, and the page says so and derives nothing. For each month and each variable the app takes the median across those cells, which is the year the sheet reads, and the 10th and 90th percentiles, which are the span the species is recorded in; the page draws both, the median as a line and the span as a band around it, on a climograph (temperature over rain, one twelve-month axis, its legend saying the band is across habitat cells and not across years), and prints them in a table beneath, stating how many cells and records the figures across the range rest on. The chart shades the three months around the coldest mean night as the cold quarter, which is a marker for the eye and not a rule the sheet uses. The rain total across cells is the 10th and 90th percentile of each cell's own annual total, never a sum of monthly percentiles, which would be a year no cell has. Radiation is CHELSA's daily-mean shortwave flux, taken to a daily total by the 86,400 seconds in a day and converted to a daily light integral (PAR taken as 45% of shortwave at 4.6 µmol per joule, so 1 MJ/m²/day ≈ 2.07 mol/m²/day), because that is the unit a grower can compare with a window or a lamp.</p>
  <p>Extremes need daily data, which CHELSA does not give, and one series, so they are read at one cell, a typical spot in the range: the one whose coldest month's mean night is nearest the median of that figure across the range (monthly means are what the grid holds; the coldest single night is a POWER figure and is read there afterwards). That typical spot is the cell nearest that median whose NASA POWER cell (0.5°) is mostly land by the same elevation layer; a cell on a coast or an island is averaged with the sea, so up to eleven further candidates, each within 2 °C of the median, are tried, and when none is mostly land the nearest stands and the page says what fraction of its POWER cell is land. The position printed for that cell is the cell's centre, never a record's coordinate. The app fetches the NASA POWER daily series for that cell's 0.5° POWER cell and reduces it: the absolute minimum, the 1st-percentile night, the 99th-percentile day, and frost nights as a count over the whole record, so one frost in forty years is printed as one night, not rounded away. POWER's cell elevation and the grid cell's ETOPO elevation differ, so the series is lapse-corrected at 6.5 °C per 1,000 m of the difference, and the page says by how much; when the grid cell's mean elevation is below sea level (a coastal cell averaged with sea floor), or when no elevation is on file for either cell, no correction is applied, and the page says which. A series is used only if it covers twenty years at 95% completeness.</p>

  <h2 id="year">What the rules read</h2>
  <p>Two fixed rules read the median year, and the sheet labels every sentence they produce as a reading. The rain rule: the wet season is the smallest set of months that carries 70% of the year's rain; a wet season whose mean is more than half a degree below the year's is called a winter growing season, more than half a degree above a summer one, and within that band neither. Eight or more wet months is no season. A temperature curve that moves less than 4 °C between its warmest and coldest month is flat, and then the rule infers no growing season even from a sharp rainy season; the page says the rain is sharp and that nothing is inferred from it, which is a different statement from "no season". Under 120 mm a year there is no rainy season to read at all, and the temperature rule names the cooler six months instead, provided they are at least a degree cooler than the other six; a flat year names nothing. A season that falls in two runs of months is printed as two runs. None of this says when the plant grows.</p>
  <p>Wherever habitat months are printed the page names the hemisphere they belong to and, where the temperature curve gives a season to reverse (not a flat year, not a habitat within 10° of the equator), gives them shifted six months for a collection in the other one, because a Namaqualand plant whose rain falls in May to August has that season in a northern greenhouse from November to February, and the difference is the whole point. The label line and the sheet format a season through one function, so they cannot disagree.</p>

  <h2 id="floor">The cold floor</h2>
  <p>The floor on a sheet is the 1st-percentile night at a typical spot in the range, from NASA POWER. When no extremes are on file (none read, the source refused or not asked, or read at a weather cell that is mostly sea and so not used), no floor is read: the coldest month's mean night in the median year from CHELSA is shown in its place, named as what it is, with a line saying it is not a floor, since a month's mean of its lows is warmer than the nights a floor is read from; a label prints no habitat night then. Where the archetype table has a conventional minimum for the plant's group and that figure is higher, the floor is the table's figure, and the same sentence says so and gives both numbers. It is a figure about a place, not a tested survival limit for a plant in a pot, and the page says so.</p>

  <h2 id="archetypes">Archetypes</h2>
  <p>The app keeps a small table of care archetypes (cactus or succulent, bulb or caudex, tropical foliage, orchid, epiphyte, fern, carnivore, temperate) resolved species → genus → family, and the page says which archetype and why it was chosen. The table supplies at most one figure, a conventional group minimum for the cold floor, and no prose; four of the eight groups (cactus or succulent, bulb or caudex, carnivore, temperate) span too much for one minimum and carry none, and the page says so for them. Where a family splits between archetypes and the genus is not on the table, the app guesses nothing.</p>

  <h2 id="notes">The note in short</h2>
  <p>The paragraph at the top of the cultivation section is the sheet's rows in one-sentence form: each row is written with its own short form by the same rule at the same moment, and the note is those sentences in card order and nothing else. There is no second rule that could disagree with a card. It lists the rows each sentence came from. It is not written by a person or a model, and the page says so.</p>
  <p>The same discipline holds on a plant's own page, where its habitat figures sit beside the place it lives in: for each compared figure the page shows the habitat median with its 10th–90th span and the place's setting side by side, and gives no verdict, because the sky over a habitat and a climate percentile are facts about places, not measured tolerances of a plant in a pot. The papers listed on a species page are works whose title or abstract names the species, most cited first; they are a pointer to the literature, and nothing on the page is drawn from them.</p>

  <h2 id="related">Similar habitat climate</h2>
  <p>A species page ends with the six species whose habitat climate is nearest its own. The distance is one number: the root of the mean squared difference, month by month, in mean day and mean night temperature, plus the difference in rain on a log scale weighted so that a factor of 2.7 in a month's rain counts as three degrees. Months are compared in calendar order, so a habitat with the same seasons six months apart is far, not near: on a bench in one hemisphere, two such plants want opposite halves of the year. Nothing else enters, not range, not family, not photographs, and the list is computed once when the index is built, from the same figures across the range the pages show. It is a statement about climates, not about plants: two species from alike climates can want different things in a pot, and the page says which figures were compared.</p>

  <p id="corpus">The reference is built on a PC and uploaded as files: one data file per species page, an index of them all, and the things the server used to work out from the index for every request, worked out once at build time and uploaded beside it under a directory named by the corpus id (a hash of the index): the index entries of each hash group, each group's figures sheets, the search split by first letter, and the catalogue's rows in each grouping. A manifest names the directory, and the server reads it first; the id is a hash of every file in the directory, which is never rewritten, so a refresh is a new directory and then a new manifest, and no cache can hold one corpus's file under another's id; a manifest whose files are not all there yet is not adopted. The server holds the index in memory and reads the rest by file, which is what lets the reference grow past what one server process could hold.</p>

  <h2 id="collection">Your collection</h2>
  <p>Everything you record lives in your browser's storage on your device as an append-only log of field-level changes, each stamped with a hybrid logical clock. What the app shows is the log read through from the start (kept as a snapshot between loads, so opening the app does not read every change again; <a href="/about/formats#snapshot">the formats page</a> says when the snapshot is dropped); two devices merge by taking the union of their logs, and for each field the latest change wins. That one rule is what backup restore and sync both use, which is why restoring an old backup over a newer collection loses nothing and why a sync interrupted halfway simply resumes. Sync encrypts every batch on the device with a key only you hold; the server stores ciphertext and cannot read a plant name. What the server does hold is a token derived from the key (it cannot be turned back into the key), sent with every request as the bearer; so the token is visible wherever request headers are, the platform included, and whoever holds it can read the ciphertext, write junk into the vault up to its limit, and take a photograph's name before your upload does, but cannot open anything. The key itself sits in this browser's storage in the clear, as the collection does, so anyone with this browser profile has both; there is no rotation and no revocation yet: "Stop syncing" forgets the key on this device and revokes nothing on the server, and a key you believe exposed means a new vault. Sync is also trust in the server's listing: a batch the server withholds, or an edit it makes to the listing, is not something a device can detect, since batches carry no chain; what it can detect is a batch altered in transit (the seal fails) and a photograph swapped (its hash is in the record). The <a href="/about/formats">formats page</a> has the details.</p>

  <h2 id="privacy">What the site knows about you</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>No accounts, no analytics, no tracking, no third-party scripts.</li>
    <li>Your collection is stored in this browser; it leaves only through sync, encrypted on the device, or a backup file you download.</li>
    <li>Your plant pages ask the server for the reference's figures by hash group (a few hundred species each), never by name.</li>
    <li>The frost watch sends your site's coordinates, rounded to about a kilometre, to this server, which asks the weather services.</li>
    <li>A search typed in the catalogue or the species picker goes to the server (the picker's goes on, from the server, to GBIF's name service); a search of your own plants stays on the device.</li>
    <li>Two small cookies: your units, and once a site is set, its hemisphere.</li>
    <li>The server keeps short-lived rate counters by address, dropped within two days, and nothing about your plants.</li>
    <li>Species pages load photographs from iNaturalist, Wikimedia Commons and GBIF's image cache, which see your address; your own pages do not unless you switch that on.</li>
  </ul>
  <details class="full"><summary>Full detail</summary>
  <p>There are no accounts, no analytics and no tracking. Species pages are served from a static corpus. Your collection, meaning its records (names, numbers, notes, dates, places, photographs), is stored in this browser and is sent nowhere unless you turn on sync, and then it is sent encrypted. What the pages about your own plants do send this site's server, and it is this list and nothing else: the hash group of each species you grow when a page needs the reference's figures (the catalogue is split into a few hundred species a group, 32 groups at its present size and more as it grows, so a species is narrowed to one in a few hundred and no further; <a href="/about/formats#privacy">the formats page</a> gives the rule); the coordinates of your site and of any place with coordinates when the frost watch asks for a forecast, with the place's altitude if one is set (rounded on the device, to a hundredth of a degree, about a kilometre, and to ten metres, before they leave it; once a site is set, the watch asks from any page: when a page opens or is returned to, every five minutes while one is in view, and at once when the site is changed in Settings; an answer is kept half an hour on the device (in this tab's session storage) and the same site is not asked again until it is that old, so the frost line can show under the top bar on every tab; a refusal, or no answer within ten seconds, is not kept, and is said as a check that did not happen); two small cookies, your units and, once a site is set, one letter for which hemisphere it is in, which goes only with species and compare pages so they render your months the right way up before any script runs; the name you are typing in the species picker, which the server answers from its own index and passes to GBIF's name service; the text you type into the catalogue's search box on the front page, which the server answers from its index (the whole index used to come to the browser for that, and no longer does), and which is the same thing a species page you then open already shows; not the text you type on "Your species", which is searched on the device, nor anything shaped like a plant number, nor anything typed before your collection has opened; the position of the catalogue's rows you scroll to or jump to by letter (a grouping and a row number, nothing of yours), which the server answers from its index; a species address the reference does not answer to, which the server asks the backbone about once, in case it is an older name for a species that is there (the answer is kept at the edge for a day); and, when you follow a link to a species page, that species, since you opened it (links on these pages are not preloaded on hover, so a name goes only when you click). No page address is sent as a referrer with any request, so a plant's number or a search never rides along with one. Those pages ask no outside host for anything unless you switch the reference's photographs on for them, in Settings or with the offer shown where a photograph is withheld, in which case the photograph is fetched from the image host named below, which then sees that this address asked for that species' picture. Your browser does talk to outside image hosts on species pages, and you should know which: photographs on species pages load from iNaturalist's image host (inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com), Wikimedia Commons (upload.wikimedia.org) and, for photographs that reached GBIF from other observation datasets, GBIF's image cache (api.gbif.org), each of which sees your address like any image host; and the species picker sends the name you are typing to this site's own <code>/api/names</code>, which asks api.gbif.org from the server, so that host sees the server's address and not yours. The frost watch asks MET Norway and, in the US, the National Weather Service from the server, never from your browser. The one thing the server keeps about a visitor is short-lived rate counters keyed by address (an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64, and for vault creation and the requests that call another service, the /48 around it too): requests per ten minutes in KV, and bytes stored per day and vaults created per day in a small counter object; every address key is dropped within two days of the day it was written (at the UTC midnight that ends the following day), the request windows a running Worker holds in memory are dropped as later requests arrive, each within ten minutes of its last use, and none holds a page, a name or a record. With sync on, the server also keeps each vault's stored size beside the vault and in the counter object, for the storage allowance, and the running count of vaults that hold something, which is a number, not a list. A write to a vault from a page on another site is refused. A photograph you remove is removed from the server too: once the removal is ten minutes old (past any Undo), a device asks the server to drop the sealed bytes, and the request carries a proof that only a holder of the sync key can make, so the bearer token alone, which can add to a vault, can never destroy in it. Two more things the device keeps outside the collection, so the list above is whole: a correction to its clock, read from the sync server's <code>Date</code> header and kept in the browser's storage (the formats page has the rule), and the place you last used on the Add form. And one path does reach the server: the address of a plant's page (<code>/plants/2026-0001</code>) is fetched from the server on the first visit after the app is installed, and again if the browser's offline cache is cleared; after that the page is served from the device without a request. The server does not log paths; the number is never in a referrer.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="glossary">Words the app uses</h2>
  <p><b>Plant</b>: one plant (or one pot of a clump) under its own permanent number, the plant number; the number is never reused, even after the plant is gone. <b>Batch</b>: a sowing or a tray of cuttings, numbered like a plant, from which plants are potted up. <b>Place</b>: a room, bench, shelf or tray, nested, with the conditions a plant inherits from it. <b>Vault</b>: the encrypted copy of your collection on this site's server, which sync keeps; it holds sealed batches of changes and photographs the server cannot read. <b>Sync key</b>: the thirty symbols that encrypt the vault and let a second device join it; there is no account behind it and no recovery. <b>Sync batch</b>: a sealed bundle of changes one device sends; also the unit a device keeps whole when it cannot read one. <b>Could not be read here</b>: a sync batch this version of the app could not read (usually from a newer version), kept whole on the sync page for a later version to read; nothing in it is lost. <b>Waiting</b>: a record on this device that lacks a field this version of the app requires (a name, a status), usually because its batch could not be read here or the file it came from never had the field; it is kept, not shown, and completes when the field arrives. <b>Refused</b>: a source that did not answer when a page was built; not the same as a source that answered "none". <b>Not reached</b>: this site could not be reached from the device just now; not the same as "not in the reference". <b>Species page</b>: the reference's page for one species, built ahead of time from the sources above. <b>Across the range</b>: across every cell of the climate grid that holds the species' in-range records; a figure across the range is the median of those cells, with the 10th to 90th percentile span. <b>A typical spot in the range</b>: the one cell whose coldest mean night is nearest the median across the range; the daily extremes, which need one weather series, are read there.</p>

  <h2 id="source">The source</h2>
  <p>Cultifolio is open source: the app under AGPL-3.0, the corpus tooling under MIT. Everything described on this page can be checked, run and rebuilt from <a href="https://github.com/zomethingje-eng/cultifolio">github.com/zomethingje-eng/cultifolio</a>, including the scripts that build a species corpus from the sources above. Problems and corrections are welcome there.</p>
